    "q-343430": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343430",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+5y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+5y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=2.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{-5(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 2 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=2{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼2{e}^{-5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
