    "q-344038": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344038",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+3{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\pi \\,\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{3}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B\\sqrt{3}\\pi +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B\\sqrt{3}\\pi  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{3}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
