    "q-343467": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343467",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $2\\pi $-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =B\\sqrt{5}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n} \\\\ B\\sqrt{5} & =4-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n} \\\\ B & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-343467-0",
